 ### EL-25a (MUST) — Outcome-sized work and resource checkpoints
 
-- **Rule:** Assign a complete independently verifiable outcome to one Goldfish owner, including analysis, test cases, implementation, targeted checks and fixes. File count, tool-use estimate or remaining token budget alone does not force a horizontal split or early handback. Use checkpoints to preserve the same outcome, owner, attempt history, evidence and next action when resources tighten. Split only into coherent vertical sub-outcomes that can each be independently verified or when a real dependency/ownership boundary requires it. An exhausted budget blocks or checkpoints that same outcome; it does not create a successor identity that resets limits. **Standard procedure:** (1) brief the whole independently verifiable outcome once; when the honest estimate exceeds the base cap, put the reason in the briefing package (`budgetRationale`) so the grant is planned, not requested after the failure (the generator refuses an estimate above the cap without it and never raises the tier on its own); (2) a checkpoint is neither a failure nor a new task: read the report and the actual diff and evidence, record a grant of up to 30 calls with a reason (`dispatch-budget-grant.mjs`) and resume the SAME agent with `SendMessage` to its id, so nothing is re-read; grants are repeatable without a lifetime limit; (3) a successor dispatch (new id) is allowed only for a genuinely new vertical outcome with its own criteria, or, with a typed reason (`elephant-restart`, `owner-lost`, `context-poisoned`, `new-vertical-outcome`), when the agent is lost or has run itself into a corner, never as budget recovery, and its briefing names the checkpointed outcome it does not continue; (4) never commit a checkpoint under the original dispatch's trailer to release the reservation and then dispatch afresh for the same outcome: that is the successor route with an extra step; (5) the briefing generator warns, never refuses, when another slice of the same item is started and unfinished (`DBG-CHECKPOINTED-OWNER`) or when the estimate exceeds the base cap without a planned grant (`DBG-ESTIMATE-ABOVE-BASE`, `DBG-ESTIMATE-NEEDS-SPLIT`); to release a slice instead of continuing it, `slice-queue.mjs cancel <id> --started --reason "<why>" --class budget`.
+- **Rule:** Assign a complete independently verifiable outcome to one Goldfish owner, including analysis, test cases, implementation, targeted checks and fixes. File count, tool-use estimate or remaining token budget alone does not force a horizontal split or early handback. Use checkpoints to preserve the same outcome, owner, attempt history, evidence and next action when resources tighten. Split only into coherent vertical sub-outcomes that can each be independently verified or when a real dependency/ownership boundary requires it. An exhausted budget blocks or checkpoints that same outcome; it does not create a successor identity that resets limits. **Standard procedure:** (1) brief the whole independently verifiable outcome once; when the honest estimate exceeds the base cap, put the reason in the briefing package (`budgetRationale`) so the grant is planned, not requested after the failure (the generator refuses an estimate above the cap without it and never raises the tier on its own); (2) a checkpoint is neither a failure nor a new task: read the report and the actual diff and evidence, record a grant of up to 30 calls with a reason (`dispatch-budget-grant.mjs`) and resume the SAME agent with `SendMessage` to its id, so nothing is re-read; grants are repeatable without a lifetime limit; (3) a successor dispatch (new id) is allowed only for a genuinely new vertical outcome with its own criteria, or, with a typed reason (`elephant-restart`, `owner-lost`, `context-poisoned`, `new-vertical-outcome`), when the agent is lost or has run itself into a corner, never as budget recovery, and its briefing names the checkpointed outcome it does not continue; (4) never commit a checkpoint under the original dispatch's trailer to release the reservation and then dispatch afresh for the same outcome and scope: that is the successor route with an extra step; (5) the briefing generator warns, never refuses, when another slice of the same item is started and unfinished (`DBG-CHECKPOINTED-OWNER`) or when the estimate exceeds the base cap without a planned grant (`DBG-ESTIMATE-ABOVE-BASE`, `DBG-ESTIMATE-NEEDS-SPLIT`); to release a slice instead of continuing it, `slice-queue.mjs cancel <id> --started --reason "<why>" --class budget`; (6) a scope decision at a checkpoint is not budget recovery: when the briefed scope turns out wrong (a check the child may not touch, a path it needs outside its scope), cancel with `--started --class wrong-premise`, land any diff it left with `goldfish-host-commit-recover.mjs plan`/`apply`, and brief a successor under a NEW id (the id binds the queue row) with the typed successor reason that fits, usually `new-vertical-outcome`; when the work is complete except an out-of-scope item, land it with the recover command without a cancel and handle the item on its own. `wait-host-commit.mjs` prints these four routes with the dispatch id at every checkpoint.
 - **Why:** Preparation defects and late test discovery are expensive; slicing analysis, tests and implementation into separate default owners loses context without improving independent acceptance. Lived example (2026-10-29): one outcome was worked as seven successor dispatches, four of which ended at the 40-call cap having only read the same large files again, and about a day of wall-clock went into briefings; the rules above were already canon and nothing refused the successor route.
 - **Check:** The dispatch ledger names one outcome and owner through targeted-green or a typed block. Any split identifies the independent criteria, evidence mapping and dependency that justify it; any checkpoint keeps the same owner and recovery pointer.
 
